@page "/group-rides/{RideId:guid}/markers/new"
@attribute [Authorize]
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Markers
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Markers
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Photos
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Rides
@using DLR.Core.Markers
@inject IApiClient Api
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>Add marker — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

@*
	Marker editor for a group ride (§16.1, §16.2).

	Two things this enforces:
	  1. Icon key is a string from the curated set, and the client renders the label
		 (§16.2). An unknown key would degrade to `note` if this UI ever hit one.
	  2. A title is optional. A pin whose icon already says "gravel" does not need the
		 word typed under it, and the overlay draws the plate alone when there is no
		 label (SkiaMapOverlay). An empty box saves as an untitled marker; the lists and
		 popups that need a name for a row fall back to the icon's own
		 (MarkerIconGlyphs.Name).

	WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: a direction. The bearing is still a field on the
	marker — it is stored, it round-trips through GPX, and the overlay rotates a pin that
	has one (§16.2's null-is-not-zero rule is unchanged, and it is the DTO and the server
	that hold it). What went is the switch-and-bearing-box pair on this screen: typing a
	number of degrees at the side of a road is not how anybody says which way a hazard
	faces, and a control nobody can use correctly is worse than no control. Markers placed
	here have no direction; ones imported from a file keep theirs.

	WHAT IS ALSO NOT HERE: a place picker. The point is chosen before this screen opens —
	"Add marker" on the live ride arms the full-screen map, and the tap that answers it
	hands the point over on the query string (see PointLatitude). This screen used to
	re-show it in a 22 rem map with a pair of coordinate boxes under it, which asked the
	rider for an answer they had just given, on a map a fifth the size of the one they gave
	it on. The point arrives decided; this screen is what goes *with* it.

	So the point is an input to this screen, not something it collects: with no lat/lon on
	the query string there is nothing to compose against, and the page says so rather than
	quietly filing the marker at some default (see _hasPoint).

	Photo attaches through PATCH /markers/{id}/photo after upload (§16.4). Web callers
	reach the file through <InputFile>; phones can also use IMediaPicker (Phase 2 keeps
	<InputFile> as the shared path — it works on every host).
*@

<div class="page page-medium form-page">
	<PageNav Title="Add marker" BackHref="@($"/group-rides/live/{RideId}")" BackLabel="Back to the adventure" />

	@if (!_hasPoint)
	{
		<p class="empty">
			This composer opens on a point you have already chosen. Open the adventure, pick
			<em>Add marker</em>, and tap the map where it goes.
		</p>

		<p><a class="button" href="@($"/group-rides/live/{RideId}")">Back to the adventure</a></p>
	}
	else
	{
		<form @onsubmit="SaveAsync" @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
			@* A grid of radios rather than a <select>: an <option> may contain text and nothing
			   else, so the moment the icons became pictures the dropdown could only have shown
			   their names. Picking a hazard marker at the side of a road is a glance-and-tap,
			   and radios keep that keyboard- and screen-reader-navigable for free. *@
			<fieldset class="icon-picker">
				@* Shut, the picker is one row: the icon that is chosen and what it is called. Tapping
				   that row opens the grid, and picking from the grid shuts it again. Twenty-nine cells
				   of artwork is the right way to answer this question and the wrong thing to leave
				   standing above the title box afterwards, on a phone, when the answer is already in.

				   The radios are rendered whether the grid is open or shut — only its display goes.
				   The checked state, the arrow-key group and the labelling stay exactly where the
				   browser and a screen reader expect them, instead of being rebuilt on every open. *@
				<button type="button" class="icon-current" @onclick="TogglePicker"
						aria-expanded="@PickerExpanded" aria-controls="icon-choices">
					<img alt="" src="@MarkerIconGlyphs.AssetPath(_icon)" />
					<span class="icon-current-name">@MarkerIconGlyphs.Label(_icon)</span>
					<span class="icon-caret" aria-hidden="true">@(_pickerOpen ? "▴" : "▾")</span>
				</button>

				<div id="icon-choices" class="icon-grid @(_pickerOpen ? "open" : "shut")">
					@foreach (MarkerIconOption option in MarkerIconGlyphs.PickerOptions)
					{
						<label class="icon-choice @(_icon == option.Key ? "chosen" : null)">
							<input type="radio" name="icon" value="@option.Key"
								   checked="@(_icon == option.Key)"
								   @onchange="() => ChooseIcon(option.Key)" />
							<img alt="" src="@MarkerIconGlyphs.AssetPath(option.Key)" />
							<span>@option.Label</span>
						</label>
					}
				</div>
			</fieldset>

			<label>
				Title <em>(optional)</em>
				<input @bind="_title" maxlength="40" placeholder="Gravel on the corner" />
			</label>
			<p class="hint">Leave it empty for a pin that is just its icon.</p>

			<label>
				Note (optional)
				<textarea @bind="_note" rows="3" maxlength="500"
						  placeholder="What is here, and what to look out for."></textarea>
			</label>

			<fieldset>
				<legend>Photo (optional)</legend>
				@if (_photo is null)
				{
					<InputFile OnChange="OnPhotoSelected" accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/heic,image/webp" />
				}
				else
				{
					<p>
						<em>@_photo.FileName selected — @(_photo.Bytes.Length / 1024) KB.</em>
						<button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="() => _photo = null">Remove</button>
					</p>
				}
				<p class="hint">
					The server re-encodes every photo and strips its metadata (§16.4) — an
					EXIF GPS tag from your driveway will not travel.
				</p>
			</fieldset>

			<button type="submit" class="primary" disabled="@_busy">
				@(_busy ? "Saving…" : "Save marker")
			</button>
		</form>

		@if (_error is not null)
		{
			<div class="error" role="alert">
				<strong>@_error.Title</strong>
				@if (_error.Messages.Count > 0)
				{
					<ul>
						@foreach (string message in _error.Messages)
						{
							<li>@message</li>
						}
					</ul>
				}
			</div>
		}
	}
</div>

@code {
	[Parameter] public Guid RideId { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// The point the author tapped on the live ride map (§16.1) — a decision the rider has
	/// already made, on a full screen of map they were reading.
	/// <para>
	/// It used to be the centre of the view they had open, which is why the composer once
	/// opened unplaced with a picker: a viewport centre is a guess, and the one point a rider
	/// never means to mark is the middle of their own screen, because that is where they are.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Required. Absent only when the composer is reached by a hand-typed or bookmarked URL,
	/// and then there is no marker to compose — see <see cref="_hasPoint"/>.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "lat")] public double? PointLatitude { get; set; }

	/// <inheritdoc cref="PointLatitude" />
	[SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "lon")] public double? PointLongitude { get; set; }

	private double _latDeg;
	private double _lonDeg;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether a point reached this screen. False renders the "nothing to compose" state
	/// instead of the form — the alternative is a composer that looks ready and files the
	/// marker at whatever the fields happened to default to, which is a wrong pin on a ride
	/// somebody is following.
	/// </summary>
	private bool _hasPoint;

	private string _icon = MarkerIcons.Fallback;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the grid of icons is showing.
	/// <para>
	/// Shut on arrival and shut again the moment a choice is made. The grid is the right way to
	/// answer "which icon" — twenty-nine pictures, all at once, glance and tap — and the wrong
	/// thing to leave standing once it is answered, because on a phone it pushes the title box
	/// and the save button off the screen for the rest of the session.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// This moves the grid's visibility and nothing else: the radios render either way, so
	/// <see cref="_icon"/> stays the single place the choice lives whether the picker is open or
	/// shut, and a screen reader keeps the same group rather than one rebuilt on each open.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _pickerOpen;

	/// <summary>
	/// <c>aria-expanded</c> for the row that opens the grid, as the literal string the attribute
	/// takes. A <see langword="bool" /> here would render the attribute away when false, and
	/// "no aria-expanded" says something different to a screen reader than "expanded=false".
	/// </summary>
	private string PickerExpanded => _pickerOpen ? "true" : "false";

	private void TogglePicker() => _pickerOpen = !_pickerOpen;

	/// <summary>Takes the choice and shuts the grid, which is what a picked list does.</summary>
	/// <param name="icon">The curated key that was tapped.</param>
	private void ChooseIcon(string icon)
	{
		_icon = icon;
		_pickerOpen = false;
	}
	private string _title = string.Empty;
	private string _note = string.Empty;
	private PickedPhoto? _photo;
	private ApiError? _error;
	private bool _busy;

	/// <summary>
	/// Quantises to the wire's resolution, so the point that arrived is exactly the one that
	/// gets stored rather than one that shifts on save.
	/// </summary>
	private static double Snap(double degrees) =>
		PositionScale.ToDegrees(PositionScale.FromDegrees(degrees));

	/// <summary>
	/// Takes the point the rider chose on the live map (§16.1) off the query string.
	/// <para>
	/// This screen no longer reads the ride to work out where to open a picker — there is no
	/// picker to open, so a ride whose markers or positions cannot be fetched no longer costs
	/// the composer two requests it does nothing with.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	protected override void OnParametersSet()
	{
		if (PointLatitude is not { } pointLat || PointLongitude is not { } pointLon)
		{
			_hasPoint = false;
			return;
		}

		_latDeg = Snap(pointLat);
		_lonDeg = Snap(pointLon);
		_hasPoint = true;
	}

	private async Task OnPhotoSelected(InputFileChangeEventArgs args)
	{
		try
		{
			IBrowserFile file = args.File;
			const long MaxBytes = 12L * 1024 * 1024;
			if (file.Size > MaxBytes)
			{
				_error = new ApiError(default, "That photo is larger than 12 MB — the server would refuse it.", Array.Empty<string>());
				return;
			}
			await using Stream stream = file.OpenReadStream(MaxBytes);
			using MemoryStream memory = new();
			await stream.CopyToAsync(memory);
			_photo = new PickedPhoto(memory.ToArray(), file.ContentType, file.Name);
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_error = new ApiError(default, "Could not read that photo.", new[] { exception.Message });
		}
	}

	private async Task SaveAsync()
	{
		// The form is not rendered without a point, so this is unreachable through the UI —
		// stated anyway, because "save whatever is in _latDeg" is the one failure on this
		// screen that produces a plausible-looking marker in the wrong place.
		if (_busy || !_hasPoint) return;
		_busy = true;
		_error = null;
		try
		{
			MarkerDto marker = await Api.CreateMarkerAsync(new CreateMarkerRequest(
				TrackId: null,
				GroupRideId: RideId,
				Lat: PositionScale.FromDegrees(_latDeg),
				Lon: PositionScale.FromDegrees(_lonDeg),
				Icon: _icon,
				Title: _title.Trim(),
				Note: string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_note) ? null : _note.Trim(),
				// Always absent from this screen — see the note at the top. Null, never zero:
				// zero is due north, and a marker that claimed one would be lying (§16.2).
				DirectionDeg: null));

			if (_photo is not null)
			{
				// Upload runs after the marker is created (§16.4). The two-step flow is what
				// lets a marker appear on the map before its photo finishes uploading, which
				// matters most in the offline case a phone hits often.
				using MemoryStream memory = new(_photo.Bytes);
				PhotoUploaded uploaded = await Api.UploadPhotoAsync(memory, _photo.ContentType, _photo.FileName);
				await Api.AttachMarkerPhotoAsync(marker.Id, new AttachPhotoRequest(uploaded.PhotoId));
			}

			Nav.NavigateTo($"/group-rides/live/{RideId}");
		}
		catch (ApiException apiException)
		{
			_error = apiException.Error;
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_error = new ApiError(default, "Could not save the marker.", new[] { exception.Message });
		}
		finally
		{
			_busy = false;
		}
	}

	private sealed record PickedPhoto(byte[] Bytes, string ContentType, string FileName);
}

<style>
	/* The icon has to carry the meaning at a glance, so it is the large half of each cell and
	   the label is the caption under it. */
	.page .icon-picker {
		border: 0;
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		padding: 0;
		min-width: 0; /* fieldset defaults to min-content, which stops the grid from wrapping */
	}

		.page .icon-picker legend {
			padding: 0;
			margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
		}

	/* Shut, the picker is this row alone: the chosen icon, its name, and a caret that says the
	   row opens. Full width and thumb-sized, because tapping it is how the grid comes back. */
	.page .icon-current {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: auto 1fr auto;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 10px;
		width: 100%;
		padding: 8px 10px;
		border: 1px solid rgb(128 128 128 / 45%);
		border-radius: 10px;
		background: transparent;
		color: inherit;
		font: inherit;
		text-align: left;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

		.page .icon-current img {
			width: 34px;
			height: 34px;
			display: block;
		}

		.page .icon-current-name {
			font-size: 0.95rem;
		}

		.page .icon-caret {
			opacity: 0.7;
		}

	/* Hidden rather than unrendered, so the radios keep the checked state and the grouping the
	   browser gives them. Three classes, so this beats the display:grid above it. */
	.page .icon-grid.shut {
		display: none;
	}

	.page .icon-grid.open {
		margin-top: 6px;
	}

	.page .icon-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(74px, 1fr));
		gap: 6px;
	}

	/* Grid rather than a flex column on purpose. The flex property that stacks children spells
	   a word this page must not contain anywhere: AddMarkerTests greps the entire rendered
	   markup, style block and all, to prove the composer offers no way to set a bearing. It is
	   a blunt guard, but it guards the right thing, so the layout gives way rather than the
	   test. Mind this when adding CSS here — and note the test name itself is unquotable. */
	.page .icon-choice {
		display: grid;
		justify-items: center;
		gap: 4px;
		padding: 8px 4px;
		border: 2px solid transparent;
		border-radius: 10px;
		cursor: pointer;
		text-align: center;
		line-height: 1.15;
		font-size: 0.72rem;
	}

		/* Visually hidden, not display:none — the radio still has to take focus and reach a
		   screen reader, which is the whole reason this is a radio group and not a div of
		   click handlers. */
		.page .icon-choice input {
			position: absolute;
			width: 1px;
			height: 1px;
			opacity: 0;
			pointer-events: none;
		}

		.page .icon-choice img {
			width: 34px;
			height: 34px;
			display: block;
		}

		.page .icon-choice.chosen {
			border-color: currentColor;
			background: rgb(128 128 128 / 18%);
		}

		.page .icon-choice:focus-within {
			outline: 2px solid currentColor;
			outline-offset: 1px;
		}
</style>
